  "query_preview": "..Increased moisture across the region will support\nthunderstorms capable of heavy rainfall rates. With relatively\nslow storm motions today, already seeing convection anchored on\nterrain training in locations and producing localized flooding of\nsmall streams and dry washes. Will need to continue to monitor\ncentral and southern Utah through the evening as rainfall rates in\nexcess of 0.50-0.75 inches per hour have been observed. The most\nprone locations will be slot canyons, normally dry washes, slick\nrock areas, areas with poor drainage and of course the numerous\nrecent burn scars across the region.\n\nAcross northwestern Utah, CAMS suggest the threat of strong to\nsevere microburst winds. One inhibiting factor will be the mid-\nmorning to early afternoon convection that moved across\nnorthwestern Utah. This likely stabilized the atmosphere a\nbit...and additional convective potential may be suppressed for\nseveral hours. While earlier runs of the HRRR showed significant\nwind gusts impacting northwestern Utah and perhaps the Wasatch \nFront in the 21Z-02Z timeframe, later runs have largely shifted \naway from this solution. Mesoanalysis correlates a fair amount of \ninhibition...and guidance suggests this CIN may not be overcome by\nthe end of peak heating.\n\nSunday looks to be a potentially interesting day convection-wise.\nA weak area of convergence associated with a Pacific trough will\nshift into northwestern Utah. Mean HREF SBCAPE values will near\n1000 J/kg across much of northern Utah by noon to 1 PM. This\ncombined with deep layer shear around 25-30 kts and general\nsynoptic scale lift will support convective development across\nmuch of Utah after noon. The most intense convection will likely \nbe across northern Utah. HREF suggests a neighborhood probability \nof wind gusts in excess of 55 mph around 15-25%, especially near \nand west of the Great Salt Lake, and 10% or so in excess of 60 \nmph. The Storm Prediction Center has outlooked this area with a \nslight risk (level 2/5) for Sunday afternoon and evening including\nthe Wasatch Front.\n\nAcross central and southern Utah, another day of thunderstorms\nwith the threat of heavy rainfall is expected. Peak rainfall rates\nin guidance are notably lower than Saturday, correlating well with\na net drying trend. That said, the threat of at least isolated\nflash flooding in the most prone locations will continue Sunday\nafternoon and evening, especially across central and southern\nUtah.\n\nThe Pacific trough will finally shift into the northern High\nPlains, shifting any large scale forcing for convection to the\neast. Given there wasn't a front to sweep out the low-level\nmoisture in the low desert areas, expect the threat of isolated\nflash flooding to persist in any areas that develop convection\nthrough at least Monday...if not longer.",
